Approach — algorithm choice (Phase 1)

The task allowed either classical CV or a lightweight learned model. **Classical CV
was chosen**, for concrete reasons rather than default:

- The detection problem is green vegetation against brown soil — separable in HSV
  colour space with no learning required.
- A learned detector (e.g. a small YOLO) would need a *training* set with plant
  bounding boxes; the 180 frames here are a *validation* set labelled with
  row-centre pixels, which is the wrong supervision for object detection and would
  need a separate annotation effort.
- Classical CV runs in single-digit milliseconds on the RTX 3050, leaving plenty of
  headroom for Week 5's closed-loop control.

The original Gazebo project's detection code was not available for direct review, so
the pipeline was rebuilt from scratch rather than ported. Even had it been available,
HSV thresholds tuned on Gazebo's flat-shaded materials would not transfer to Isaac's
RTX-rendered colours without full recalibration — so a rebuild was the honest call.

Synthetic dataset (Phase 2)

Generated with Isaac Sim Replicator (`generate_dataset.py`), headless.

- **180 frames** = 5 rows × 12 positions along each row × 3 lighting levels
  (sun intensity 1200 / 1600 / 2200).
- **Ground truth without manual labelling.** Because the field is procedural, every
  row's world position is known. For each frame the row centreline is projected
  through the camera's real view/projection matrices into pixel space — that
  projected column is the ground-truth row centre. No hand-labelling.
- **Lateral drift added.** A camera sitting perfectly on its row every frame gives a
  constant ground truth (image centre) and a useless validation set — a detector that
  always outputs "centre" would score perfectly. To make the set meaningful, the
  camera is offset sideways off the row by a random ±0.0–0.6 m per frame, so the row
  appears off-centre and ground truth varies (≈514–766 px across the drift range).
- Output: `frames/NNNN.png`, `manifest.csv` (frame → row, drift, lighting,
  ground-truth pixel), and `overlay_preview/` frames with the ground-truth line drawn
  for visual verification.

**Not varied:** row curvature. The field has straight rows only, so curved-row
robustness is untested and is listed as a limitation.

Detection pipeline (Phase 3)

`detect_row.py` — HSV threshold → column-histogram row selection.

- **HSV bounds calibrated on the actual frames**, not guessed. A helper
  (`calibrate_hsv.py`) sampled vegetation vs soil pixels from the rendered frames and
  reported the green cluster (hue ≈ 47–61) cleanly separated from soil (hue ≈ 0–25).
  Final bounds: lower `[42, 75, 49]`, upper `[66, 255, 255]`.
- **Row selection.** An early version averaged all green columns across the width.
  That worked when the row was centred but failed badly on drifted frames: with a
  neighbouring row also in view, the average was dragged toward it. Replaced with a
  column-intensity histogram of the green mask over the lower (near-field) region;
  each visible row is a peak, and the pipeline picks the peak nearest the expected
  position (image centre for a following camera), then refines with a local centroid.
- Output: detected row-centre pixel and a detection flag. Overlays saved with the
  detected centre (red) and image centre (cyan) for inspection.
- Two modes: `batch` (over the dataset → `detections.csv`) and `live` (subscribes to
  `/robot/camera/image_raw`, overlays detection in real time for the recording).

Baseline metrics (Phase 4)

Scored `pred_center_px` against `gt_center_px` (`evaluate.py`), over all 180 frames.

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Detection rate | 180 / 180 = 100 % |
| Mean offset error | 105 px |
| Median offset error | 66 px |
| 90th-percentile error | 198 px |
| Max error | 727 px |
| Processing time | 2.64 ms/frame (≈ 380 FPS) |

**Error by lateral drift** — this is the important slice:

| \|drift\| (m) | frames | mean err (px) | median (px) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.00 | 26 | 3.9 | 2.5 |
| 0.15 | 42 | 41.9 | 40.6 |
| 0.35 | 42 | 94.3 | 81.5 |
| 0.60 | 70 | 186.8 | 167.4 |

Lighting had almost no effect (mean 88–127 px across all three levels), which
confirms the HSV threshold is robust to illumination. Error is driven almost entirely
by drift.

What works, what doesn't (Phase 5)

**Works well:**
- On-row and small-drift frames (≤ 0.15 m): error within ~40 px, well inside usable
  range for a controller.
- Illumination robustness: performance flat across the tested lighting range.
- Speed: ~380 FPS leaves large headroom for closed-loop control.

**Degrades / known limitations:**
- **Large drift (≥ 0.35 m):** error climbs sharply. At 0.6 m the target row sits near
  the frame edge and a *neighbouring* row can be closer to image centre, so
  "peak nearest centre" picks the wrong row. This is a genuine single-frame ambiguity;
  the standard fix is temporal tracking (using the previous frame's estimate as the
  prior — the `prior_cx` hook is already in the detector), which belongs with Week 5's
  controller rather than in a per-frame detector.
- **Curvature untested:** the field has straight rows only.
- **Sparse dead plants:** the generated field came out predominantly healthy, so the
  dead/wilting failure mode (brown plants invisible to a green mask) is described
  analytically rather than measured. A field variant with more dead plants would be
  needed to quantify it.
